CAPABILITY EVIDENCE  (why TEST_EXIT=1)
------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
The prescribed gate command `dotnet test apps/windows/TeslaSync.sln` includes TeslaSync.App.UITests, a
WinAppDriver/Appium UI-automation suite that drives the packaged app over http://127.0.0.1:4723. This
runner has no such automation runner:
    Test-NetConnection 127.0.0.1:4723        => open    : False
    WinAppDriver.exe (standard paths / PATH) => present : False
By design (apps/windows/TeslaSync.App.UITests/README.md: "every test fails fast with an explicit reason;
it is never silently skipped, and the gate ends STATUS=BLOCKED") all 147 UITests raise
UiAutomationUnavailableException ("WinAppDriver/Appium runner is absent: nothing is listening on
http://127.0.0.1:4723/ and WinAppDriver.exe was not found ..."), so TEST_EXIT=1.

This is a PRE-EXISTING, surface-independent runner gap that fails the 147 UITests of EVERY surface
identically — it is not caused by the DrivingTemperatureStats code. The capabilities this surface
actually requires (WinUI 3 / Windows App SDK 1.6+ / .NET 10 / Fluent) ARE present on the runner: the app
project builds green and produces TeslaSync.App.dll, and the full headless suite is green. Same finding
and verdict as sibling 0041 ResponseViewer.

------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
PRE-EXISTING BASELINE REPAIR  (disclosed for honesty; tightly coupled — the test gate could not run otherwise)
------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
apps/windows/TeslaSync.App.Tests/TeslaSync.App.Tests.csproj was committed-at-HEAD in a NON-LOADABLE state
(MSB4025) before any DrivingTemperatureStats work, so build+test were red for every surface:
  1. A ClientUtilitiesSection -> CronParser -> TirePressureVisualWidget region had collapsed into a tangle
     of nested/unterminated XML comments (a stray `<!--` inside a comment) plus TWO duplicate
     TirePressureVisualWidget <Compile> triples (would also trip NETSDK1022). Removed the whole corrupted
     block; the canonical TirePressureVisualWidget triple remains linked exactly once in its own ItemGroup.
  2. Three feature-view groups (LiveSignalsTable, XRayFieldsTable, RateLimitStatusPanel) had lost their
     `</ItemGroup>` close + opening `<!--`, leaving three unbalanced <ItemGroup> tags (the MSB4025 the
     loader reported at `</Project>`). Restored the three `</ItemGroup>` + `<!--` separators.
  Result: well-formed XML, balanced 40 `<!--` / 40 `-->` and 37 `<ItemGroup>` / 37 `</ItemGroup>`, zero
  duplicate Compile items, UTF-8/no-BOM/LF preserved. (Same class of repair disclosed by sibling 0041.)

